    card title "The apprentice, and the news"

So for a lender, the sign hangs on sigma. Now swi (@run code sim values.savings [100,50,0] values.r [2,2,2] values.sigma [0.5,0.5,0.5]@)tch to Ingrid, who borrows today.
    draw axes line_1 line_2 sim sim_ctl_r sim_ctl_sigma sim_ctl_savings rd_c0 rd_pct parallel true

B: Same twenty-six thousand a year at two percent. Her money just arrives later.
    draw who

Raise the rate to three percent, sigma still a half, and her spending today f (@highlight rd_pct effect circle@)alls eight percent.
    run code sim values.savings [0,0,0] values.r [2,2.5,3] values.sigma [0.5,0.5,0.5]

B: Substitution, as before. And the interest on what she  (@draw tally_line_2@)owes goes up, so the income effect turns against her.
    draw tally_box tally_line_1

And a third push: the pay she is counting on  (@draw tally_line_3@)is worth less in today's money when the future is discounted at three percent. Call it the wealth effect.

All three point down. Sweep sigma from almost nothing to two, and she cuts every time: four percent at the bottom, twenty-four at the top.
    run code sim values.savings [0,0,0,0,0] values.r [3,3,3,3,3] values.sigma [0.1,0.5,1,1.5,2]

    erase tally

Your turn: slide her savings from none to all, and find the household who doesn't react at all.
    explore code sim

    erase axes line_1 line_2 sim sim_ctl_r sim_ctl_sigma sim_ctl_savings rd_c0 rd_pct who

B: Wait, though. Central banks signal their moves months ahead. Rates rarely jump by surprise.
    draw re_x re_y re_xl re_yl re_t0 re_t5 re_t10 re_t15 parallel true

Then a rational planner has already built the rise into her plan. Say Ingrid lea (@draw re_rise re_riselab@)rns today that rates go to four percent in five years.

She trims spending at once, to about twenty-three thousand, and when the rise arrives, nothing jumps.
    path re_ant #2f6b8f points [[120,300],[173,300],[227,300],[280,300],[333,300],[387,310],[440,325],[493,340],[547,350],[600,365],[653,380],[707,395],[760,410],[813,420],[867,436],[920,451]]
    text re_antlab "announced: $23k now" #2f6b8f x 653 y 453 font_size 36

If it comes as a surprise, she spends twenty-six thousand for five years, then cuts sharply.
    path re_sur #b5482e points [[120,486],[173,486],[227,486],[280,486],[333,486],[360,486],[360,254],[387,254],[440,269],[493,279],[547,295],[600,305],[653,320],[707,335],[760,345],[813,360],[867,375],[920,390]]
    text re_surlab "surprise: $26k" #b5482e x 237 y 523 font_size 36

B: So spending moves when the news arrives, not when the rate does.
    text re_news "spending moves with the news" x 620 y 230 font_size 40

## How willing are we?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: You'd make a fine central-bank economist.
    good: 'Mostly right: the borrowers are the easy part.'
    poor: 'Rates are slippery: savers and borrowers react differently.'
    none: Even central banks argue about this one.
sources:
    - id: havranek2015jeea
      title: 'Measuring Intertemporal Substitution: The Importance of Method Choices and Selective Reporting'
      authors: Tomáš Havránek
      year: 2015
      finding: '2,735 estimates from 169 studies; selective reporting (dropping negative and insignificant estimates) inflates the mean; corrected mean about 0.3–0.4 for micro estimates of asset holders and about zero for macro estimates; calibrations above 0.8 inconsistent with the bulk of evidence. Journal of the European Economic Association 13(6): 1180–1204.'
    - id: havranek2015jie
      title: Cross-country heterogeneity in intertemporal substitution
      authors: Tomáš Havránek, Roman Horváth, Zuzana Iršová, Marek Rusnák
      year: 2015
      finding: 'The same 2,735 estimates across 104 countries; the mean reported estimate is about 0.5; larger in richer countries and where more households hold stocks. Journal of International Economics 96(1): 100–118.'
    - id: hall1988
      title: Intertemporal Substitution in Consumption
      authors: Robert E. Hall
      year: 1988
      finding: 'Twentieth-century US aggregate data show no strong evidence that the elasticity of intertemporal substitution is positive. Journal of Political Economy 96(2): 339–357.'
    - id: gruber2013
      title: A Tax-Based Estimate of the Elasticity of Intertemporal Substitution
      authors: Jonathan Gruber
      year: 2013
      finding: Using variation in capital income tax rates across households (Consumer Expenditure Survey), an EIS of about 2. Quarterly Journal of Finance 3(1).

