    text hall "aggregate data ≈ 0\n(Hall 1988)" #2f6b8f cites ["hall1988"] x 170 y 235 font_size 32 hidden true
    text gruber "Gruber 2013: ≈ 2" #8a5fa8 cites ["gruber2013"] x 810 y 500 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st1 "σ < 1 for most: MOSTLY AGREED" #2f6b8f x 330 y 640 font_size 32 hidden true
    text st2 "exact size: CONTESTED" #b5482e x 760 y 640 font_size 32 hidden true
    path hall_tick thick #2f6b8f points [[150,375],[150,290]] hidden true

    card title "How willing are we?"

Everything so far hung on one number, sigma. For a saver, below one a rate rise means more spending today; above one, less.
    draw sscale flip fliplab

    pause 0.3

    ask "Where does the average published estimate of sigma lie? Click on the line." on sscale store g after keep voice b
        intro: So which is it for real people? Guess first.
        right: Good guess: {g}. The average reported estimate is about a half.
        wrong: You said {g}; the average reported estimate is about a half.

Studies report about  (@draw mean@)a half on average. Tomáš Havránek gathered 2,735 estimates from 169 published studies.

B: And he found that researchers too often leave out estimates that come out negative or insignificant.
    highlight mean effect underline

Correct for that (@draw band bandlab@), and household data on people who hold assets give about 0.3 to 0.4. Aggregate (@draw hall_tick hall@) data give about zero, close to what Robert Hall found in 1988.

B: Not everyone agrees. Jonathan Gruber used differences in how peop (@draw gruber@)le's interest is taxed and found about two.

How sure are we? That sigma is  (@draw st1@)below one for most people is mostly agreed; it (@draw st2@)s exact size is contested.

So the 'save more' pull is weaker than the advice assumes: for borrowers a rise bites, and for savers it may barely matter.
    highlight flip fliplab

    quiz "A family with steady pay has a big variable-rate mortgage and no savings. The real rate rises unexpectedly, for good. What happens to their spending today?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: It falls: their debt now costs more, which makes them poorer, and later has become cheaper too, so both effects push down.
        wrong: They owe money: dearer debt makes them poorer, and the pull toward later points the same way: here, it falls.
        * It rises
        + It falls
        * No change

    quiz "Suppose Gruber is right and sigma is about 2 for most people. A surprise rate rise hits a town of retirees living off their savings. What happens to their spending this year?" voice b
        right: It falls: above one, the pull toward later beats the gain in interest income.
        wrong: For savers, sigma one is the flip point, and two is above it: here, it falls.
        * It rises
        + It falls
        * No change

    quiz "A central bank said a year ago that it would raise rates today, and today it does exactly that. In the model, what happens to spending today?"
        right: About nothing: the plan changed a year ago, when the news came.
        wrong: When did the planner learn? A year ago: here, about nothing.
        * A big cut
        * A big rise
        + About nothing

So how much should you change your spending when rates rise? If you saw it coming, you already have. If it's news: less if you borrow, maybe even more if you live off savings. Whether real people plan this carefully is the next question.
    highlight st1 st2

